const body = `<h1>1.8b — Compound Assignment &amp; The Overflow Bug</h1>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Your combat loop has been doing damage for a whole unit. You have probably
        written <code>hp = hp - damage;</code> hundreds of times. Today you meet the
        shorthand, and then something stranger: a number that breaks.
    </p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Experiment: The Shorthand Family</h3>
        <p>Create <code>CompoundLab.java</code>. Predict every line first:</p>
        <pre><code>int gold = 100;
gold += 50;    System.out.println(gold);   // ?
gold -= 25;    System.out.println(gold);   // ?
gold *= 2;     System.out.println(gold);   // ?
gold /= 3;     System.out.println(gold);   // ?
gold %= 7;     System.out.println(gold);   // ?</code></pre>
        <p>Run it. Then prove to yourself that <code>gold += 50</code> and
        <code>gold = gold + 50</code> are the same thing by writing both forms side by
        side with different variables.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>The pattern:</strong> <code>x op= y</code> means <code>x = x op y</code>
        for the operators <code>+ - * / %</code>. The exam expects you to read these
        fluently. Also in the family: <code>x++</code> (add 1) and <code>x--</code>
        (subtract 1), which you have been using in loop counters for weeks.
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Experiment 2: The Gold Doubler</h3>
        <p>A treasure room doubles your gold every time you enter. Model it:</p>
        <pre><code>int gold = 1;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 40; i++) {
    gold *= 2;
}
System.out.println("Gold after 40 rooms: " + gold);</code></pre>
        <p>Before running: gold should be huge. Predict the shape of the number. Then run
        it. Write down exactly what prints. Is it positive? Did anything strange happen
        around a specific room count? Try 30 rooms, then 31, 32, 33. Find the exact
        room where the number goes wrong.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Overflow.</strong> An <code>int</code> can only count so high. The
        biggest one is <code>Integer.MAX_VALUE</code>, which is 2,147,483,647 (about
        2.1 billion). Past the top, the number <strong>wraps around to negative</strong>.
        This is not a crash. It is not an error message. Your program keeps running with
        a wrong number, silently. That is what makes overflow dangerous in real systems:
        nothing warns you.
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Experiment 3: Meet the Edges</h3>
        <pre><code>System.out.println(Integer.MAX_VALUE);
System.out.println(Integer.MAX_VALUE + 1);
System.out.println(Integer.MIN_VALUE);
System.out.println(Integer.MIN_VALUE - 1);</code></pre>
        <p>Predict all four — write them down. Run it. Did the last two come out
        the way you expected? Explain in your notes why they look the way they do.
        (Hint: think of an odometer rolling over.)</p>
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The Bank That Went Negative</h3>
        <p>From the <a href="../docs/unit-01/compound-assignment.html">compound
        assignment docs</a>: three short programs, each with exactly one bug. Find it,
        predict the wrong output, fix it, prove the fix. The third one is an overflow
        bug hiding inside a perfectly reasonable-looking loot bonus.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Rewrite <code>hp -= damage * 2</code> in long form. Careful: the whole
            right side is computed first.</li>
            <li>Your RPG tracks total damage dealt over a whole campaign. A very long
            campaign might deal billions of damage. Is <code>int</code> safe here?
            What type would you reach for instead?</li>
            <li><code>int x = 5; int y = x++;</code> vs <code>int x = 5; int y = ++x;</code>
            Run both. What is y in each? The difference is pre vs post increment. The
            exam tests this.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Read and write <code>+= -= *= /= %=</code> fluently</li>
            <li>State <code>Integer.MAX_VALUE</code> and explain overflow wraparound</li>
            <li>Predict when a loop-based calculation will overflow</li>
            <li>Explain the difference between <code>x++</code> and <code>++x</code> in
            an assignment</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/compound-assignment.html">Compound Assignment &amp; Range of Variables</a> — the shorthand table, overflow, the edge cases</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/variables.html">Variables &amp; Types</a> — what an int can hold</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a> — Integer.MIN_VALUE / MAX_VALUE are printed on your exam sheet</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
